> The New Mexico[ Museum] of Art, in partnership with The Philbrook[ Museum] of Art, Tulsa, OK, presents the dynamic and psychologically[ penetrating] watercolor paintings of Cady Wells (1904-1954). This group of more than 25 works features Wells’[ uniquely] modernist interpretations of Southwestern landforms and cultural-religious[ traditions].
Born to a traditional, well-to-do New England family, Wells settled in northern New Mexico[ beginning] in[ 1932]. There, his art took on the complex layering of a[ spirit] inspired by music, calligraphy and stained glass, but traumatized by active WWII combat, sexual intolerance, and Atomic bomb experiments at Los Alamos, just 12 miles from where he lived and painted. Such mid-[century] influences marked his increasingly[ surreal]ist style with equal parts rapture and dis[quiet]ude.

